                DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION DONATES
        INTERNET ALPHA SYSTEMS FOR MIT MEDIA LAB CELEBRATION


MAYNARD, Mass., October 12, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that it donated high-performance AlphaStation 
workstations, ATM products, and Celebris XL Personal Workstations 
valued at more than $500,000, used at the 10th anniversary 
celebration of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology's Media 
Lab, "10/10: A Day in the Life of Cyberspace event." 

     The 10/10 event marks the tenth anniversary of the MIT media 
lab on October 10.  MIT began celebration of the anniversary over 
the first 10 days in October by highlighting different cultural 
aspects of Cyberspace.  The event was designed to expand the 
frontiers of digital communications.

     Editors collected, edited and published reflections on the new 
digital age, submitted by millions of people during the 10 days 
prior to the event.  Highlights included:  Portraits in Cyberspace, 
an internationally juried digital art exhibition; Voices from the 
Ether, a collage of spoken stories gathered by global 800 numbers;  
Bits to the Future, a "digital rosetta stone" of messages for 
tomorrow, in all major languages; and Beyond the Fringe, the past, 
present and future edge of the art from the MIT Media Lab. Many of 
the stories and portraits gathered will become the basis of a book, 
and will become part of a digital time capsule chronicling the 
theme, "A Day in the Life of Cyberspace."

     Several AlphaStation workstations and AlphaServer systems as 
well as Celebris XL Personal Workstations were used as Web browsing 
stations, file servers and multimedia object stations. These systems 
were connected to two terabytes of disk storage containing the 
collected multimedia bits contributed from all over the world.  All 
of the hardware is interconnected using Digital's ATM adapters to 
take advantage of MIT's high bandwidth network.

     Digital's AlphaStation workstations and AlphaServer systems are 
based on high-performance 64-bit RISC technology and offer support 
for all major operating system environments--Digital UNIX, OpenVMS 
and Windows NT.  The Celebris XL is Digital's line of industry 
standard personal workstations ranging from 100MHz to 133MHz systems 
with the flexibility of upgrading to a 233MHz or 266MHz Alpha 
processor.  The ATMworks 750 adapter is a turbochannel to ATM 
connection for Digital's high-performance Alpha systems. This allows 
MIT's clients and servers to take advantage of their high bandwidth 
ATM network, relieve information bottlenecks and speed data flow.

     "It was an absolute pleasure to develop new communications 
technology for the 10/10 celebration on Digital's Alpha platform, 
the fastest and most flexible computing environment in the world," 
said Andy Lippman, associate director of the MIT Media Lab. 
"Digital's Alpha systems are the backbone of our Media Bank for 
future projects."

     "We will continue to work with researchers at the MIT Media Lab 
as we explore future Internet communication and multimedia 
technologies. The goal is that it leads to new products and services 
for Digital as a result of our joint research," said Jeanne Lyons, 
Research Business Development Manager in Digital's Advanced 
Technology Group.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
